menu "pinled configuration"

    menu "GPIO assignment (ESP32-S3 defaults)"
        config PINLED_CLK_GPIO
            int "74x161 CLK GPIO (shared bus)"
            default 18
            help
                A0 on the QT Py ESP32-S3; IO18 on the S3-DevKitC-1.
        config PINLED_MR_GPIO
            int "/MR reset GPIO (-1 = not fitted)"
            default 17
            range -1 48
            help
                A1 on the QT Py ESP32-S3; IO17 on the S3-DevKitC-1.

                /MR is bussed to every module and asynchronously clears all
                counters and both '109 latches. It is pin 5 of the 5-pin
                harness. See docs/CHAINING.md.
        config PINLED_DATA_GPIO
            int "DATA_IN GPIO (shared by every module on the chain)"
            default 9
            help
                A2 on the QT Py ESP32-S3; IO9 on the S3-DevKitC-1.

                All modules bus their tri-state '251 outputs onto this one
                line; only the addressed module drives it. This is not
                per-module — chaining costs no additional GPIO.
        config PINLED_LED_GPIO
            int "WS2812B/SK6812 data GPIO (RMT)"
            default 8
            help
                A3 on the QT Py ESP32-S3; IO8 on the S3-DevKitC-1.

        # Note: the POC defaults (25/27/26/15) are unusable here. GPIO 26 and 27
        # are SPI flash pins on the ESP32-S3, and 15 and 25 are not broken out
        # on this board.
    endmenu

    menu "Scan geometry"
        config PINLED_NUM_MODULES
            int "Number of 16-channel modules on the bus"
            range 1 8
            default 1
        config PINLED_CHANNELS_PER_MODULE
            int "Channels per module (8 = single mux, 16 = dual '251)"
            range 8 16
            default 16
        config PINLED_ACTIVE_LOW
            bool "Front end is active-low (inverting)"
            default n
            help
                The v2 front end is a level-shift MOSFET (inverting) followed by
                an inverting Schmitt trigger, so it is non-inverting overall:
                lamp on reads as logic high. Leave this off for v2 hardware.

                This must stay consistent with the orientation of the DATA_IN
                bias resistor: a floating or unpopulated bus has to read "lamp
                off", which for a non-inverting front end means a pull-down.
                lamp_scan also derives the MCU's internal pull on DATA_IN from
                this setting, so all three flip together.
                See docs/TIMING.md section 4.2.
    endmenu

    menu "Chain clock (SPI)"
        config PINLED_SPI_HZ
            int "Chain clock rate (Hz)"
            default 4000000
            range 100000 10000000
            help
                SCLK rate for the scan burst. 4 MHz gives a 250 ns bit slot,
                against ~100 ns of accumulated clock skew across 8 LVC modules
                (docs/TIMING.md section 4.3).

                4 MHz rather than 2 because a frame carries ~17 us of fixed
                transaction overhead on top of the burst (measured). At 2 MHz
                a 128-channel frame is 87 us, which the boot check clamps to
                below the 10 kHz target; at 4 MHz it is 52 us and fits.

                Raising this shortens the frame and buys idle-gap margin;
                lowering it is the first thing to try if far modules read wrong
                while near ones are fine.

        config PINLED_SPI_MODE
            int "SPI mode (0 or 1)"
            default 0
            range 0 1
            help
                Leave this at 0 for production hardware.

                A module Schmitt-inverts its incoming clock, so the '161 counts
                on the FALLING edge and line n is valid from fall(n) to
                fall(n+1). Mode 0 (CPOL=0, CPHA=0) samples on rising edges --
                the middle of that window, with margin on both sides.

                Mode 1 samples on the falling edge, which is exactly the counter
                transition. It is wrong for this hardware.

                Measured caveat: on a bench rig that clocks the '161 directly,
                the counter advances on the same rising edges SPI samples, and
                NEITHER mode aligns -- every sample reads line[k+1] and line 0
                is unreachable. Add an inverter in the CLK path to make such a
                rig behave like production.

        config PINLED_MR_FROM_CS
            bool "Drive /MR from the SPI CS line"
            default y
            help
                Wires /MR to the SPI peripheral's chip-select with positive
                polarity, so it is LOW between transactions (clearing the
                chain) and HIGH during (counting). The frame reset becomes
                hardware-timed and cannot be forgotten or mistimed.

                Turn off to drive /MR as a plain GPIO pulsed before each
                frame -- useful on a rig where CS is not routed to the
                counter's clear pin.

        config PINLED_ARM_CLOCK
            bool "Modules arm on their first clock (rev C hardware)"
            default y
            help
                On rev C a module's ENP comes from its STARTED latch. Because
                ENP is a synchronous enable, the first clock edge sets STARTED
                while the counter samples the old 0 and stays put -- so the
                module spends one clock arming and the next sixteen presenting
                lines. That is what gives line 0 a full bit period.

                A frame is therefore 17 clocks per module and every 17th sample
                is discarded.

                Turn OFF for a legacy bench rig whose '161 has ENP tied high:
                there the first clock counts, so an arm clock would shift every
                channel by one.
    endmenu

    menu "Timing"
        config PINLED_SAMPLE_RATE_HZ
            int "Per-channel raw sample rate (Hz)"
            default 10000
            help
                Target rate, paced by a hardware timer so dt is uniform --
                which is what the filament integrator assumes.

                This is a request, not a promise: at boot the driver measures
                what the scan hardware actually sustains and clamps to 60% of
                the free-run ceiling. The clamped value is what every time
                constant is derived from. See docs/TIMING.md section 2.6.
        config PINLED_REFRESH_HZ
            int "LED frame rate (Hz)"
            range 30 240
            default 90
    endmenu

    menu "Filament model defaults"
        config PINLED_ATTACK_MS
            int "Filament attack time constant (ms)"
            default 30
        config PINLED_DECAY_MS
            int "Filament decay time constant (ms)"
            default 40
    endmenu

    menu "LED string"
        config PINLED_LED_COUNT
            int "Number of addressable LEDs"
            default 16
    endmenu

    menu "Diagnostics"
        config PINLED_SCAN_DEBUG
            bool "Log raw scan frames (bring-up aid)"
            default y
            help
                Prints the raw pre-filament channel bitmap twice a second, plus
                a per-channel flag showing whether that channel has ever been
                observed both high and low since boot.

                Use this to prove the scan bus is alive independently of the
                filament model and the LED string: press a test input and watch
                the corresponding bit move. A channel that never toggles is
                unconnected, unpopulated, or pinned by the bus bias.

                Turn off for production -- it costs an array write per channel
                per frame inside the scan loop.

        config PINLED_SCAN_STEP_MS
            int "Slow-step the scan (ms per channel, 0 = normal speed)"
            default 0
            range 0 2000
            help
                Bring-up only. At normal speed the scan runs tens of thousands
                of frames per second, so the '161 Q outputs toggle far too fast
                to watch and indicator LEDs on them just look steadily lit.

                Set this to e.g. 250 and the scan advances one channel every
                250 ms instead, logging the count, the Q pattern to expect, and
                the DATA_IN level at each step. The counter then walks slowly
                enough to read by eye or follow with a meter.

                While non-zero, scan_task is NOT started -- the slow walk owns
                the counter -- so the filament model and the LED string do not
                update. Set back to 0 for normal operation.

        config PINLED_SPI_SWEEP
            bool "Sweep the chain clock and report where it breaks"
            default n
            help
                Bring-up only. Steps the SPI clock through a fixed ladder and,
                at each rate, reads a burst of frames and reports how many were
                identical plus the union of every bit seen.

                Hold one test input down and let it run: a rate is good when the
                burst is 100% stable and the union shows only that one channel.
                Instability or extra bits in the union is the ceiling.

                This is TIMING.md section 7 item 3 -- the clock-skew estimate is
                datasheet arithmetic and wants measuring. Suppresses scan_task,
                so the LEDs do not update.

        config PINLED_SCAN_HOLD_CH
            int "Park the counter on one channel (-1 = disabled)"
            default -1
            range -1 127
            help
                Bring-up only, and takes precedence over the slow-step walk.

                Pulses /MR, clocks the counter to this channel, then stops and
                never clocks again, sampling DATA_IN at 2 Hz. The address lines
                hold a fixed value, so every node in the chain becomes a static
                DC level you can measure with an ordinary meter:

                  - '161 Q outputs   -> the channel number in binary
                  - '151 A/B/C       -> the low three bits of it
                  - '151 D<n>        -> your input, follow it with the button
                  - '151 Y (pin 5)   -> should equal D<n> while /E is low
                  - the MCU DATA pin -> should equal Y

                Whichever link in that chain stops tracking is the fault. Set
                back to -1 for normal operation.
    endmenu

endmenu
